Should probably call synctest.Wait() within each test as opposed to
being called within some goroutine's call stack.  This should make the
synchronization point more obvious; the "system" has stabilized and it is
safe to examine data after synctest.Wait() returns.

Tests may need to end with a time.Sleep(1*time.Second) or similar.  The
synctest package will advance time to allow a goroutine to run once all
the goroutines in the current bubble are durably blocked.  This should
allow any sleeping goroutines to finish before the main goroutine returns
and (potentially) panics because there are still goroutines associated
with the bubble.  Another option appears to be synctest.Sleep(), but that
apparently came in with go 1.27, and I am currently using go1.25.9.

The "server code" should probably be called in a goroutine.  This allows
the TestXXX function to simulate the client(s) independently of the server
goroutine.

Instead of doing a struct literal, use a "newXXX" function.  The newXXX
function can be extended to add null/stub functions as the functionality
of the structure is extended.  Adding new null/stub functions helps ensure
existing code runs essentially the same as before; new test can update the
stub function(s) to something that can either capture data or control the
"injection" of data.

Need to evaluate this one, but it may make sense to have all the tests
written without the "Test" prefix, and have one TestX() that iterates over
a table of the functions to be run like:
	for _, f := range table {
		synctest.Test(t, f)
	}
One downside is it may be "too easy" to accidentally disable/skip a test.
Another downside may be that we lose the control we have with the
XXX argument in the "go test -run XXX" command.

